===Etymology 2===
From {{der|is|non|vinna}}, from {{der|is|gem-pro|*winnaną}}, from {{der|is|ine-pro|*wen-||to strive, wish, desire, love}}. Related to {{cog|fo|vinna}}, {{cog|sv|vinna}},  {{cog|nb|vinne}}, {{cog|en|win}}, {{cog|nl|winnen}}, and {{cog|de|gewinnen}}.

====Verb====
{{is-verb-strong|vann|unnu|unnið}}

# {{lb|is|intransitive}} to [[work]], to [[labour]]{{jump|is|to work, to labour|s}}
#: {{ux|is|''Ég nenni ekki að '''vinna'''.''|I don't want to '''work'''.}}
#: {{ux|is|''Ég '''vinn''' allan daginn.''|I '''work''' all day.}}
# {{lb|is|transitive|with accusative}} to [[do]], to [[perform]] (e.g. a task){{jump|is|to do, to perform|s}}
# {{lb|is|intransitive}} to [[win]], to achieve [[victory]] {{jump|is|to win, to achieve victory|s}}
#: {{ux|is|''Ég '''vann'''!''|I '''won'''!}}
# {{lb|is|transitive|with accusative}} to [[win]], to [[triumph]] in (e.g. a game)
#: {{ux|is|''Við '''unnum''' keppnina.''|We '''won''' the tournament.}}
# {{lb|is|transitive|with accusative}} to [[conquer]], to [[beat]], to [[defeat]]{{jump|is|to conquer|s}}
#: {{ux|is|''Fjölnismenn '''unnu''' Val 2–1 í gær.''|[[w:Ungmennafélagið Fjölnir|Fjölnir]] '''beat''' [[w:Valur Football Club|Valur]] 2–1 yesterday.}}
# {{lb|is|transitive|with accusative}} to [[win]], to [[obtain]], to [[get]]{{jump|is|to win, to obtain|s}}
#: {{ux|is|''Herinn '''vann''' borgina frá uppreisnarmönnunum.''|The army '''took''' the city from the rebels.}}

